","9f6edbfc","2fb5161c","91a0f839","7803cdcd","38446d4f","4c9ac78e","498afc59","37399938","1d9cc1d2","50fcdf64","9fa8c2d6","9e976aa4","a0421f78","d57e0f49","a2954bde","3ff291bc","9525c2f8","766d421c","e60697c9","71facfdd","24ab2814","7316778f","8c25a244","450e96bc","bdad5e42","e23cd431","5b859b74","6719aecb","f0d13426","fe448198","b4563ce7","ffed871d","3bd729f8","d8252a57","5dd874e9","0435f7ca","680416a5","40e240df","b45f933e","a644c0e8","9c0a44b9","5b2069ff","5bd1d7a5","2d884c85","ecd42907","a7f4a096","eeaa1a34","62c6dd1d","b170dc6a","45ec9801","4be1bc1e","da24b668","014bb688","8cf051c8","4769dc48","c860f2b4","e722d236","efbe4da7","41364b3c","3259258f","bbdee90d","9517fd46","5294f6fa","b121ebca","e4eb3e4c","71fdea0e","3cf453db","1e32f783","d299a782","bc7507cd","92a556a5","481195c3","61a19938","0b4fdc07"]},"lines":[{"id":"0.0","chapter":0,"index":0,"startFen":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","steps":[{"san":"e4","uci":"e2e4","fenBefore":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1","fenAfter":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1","key":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -|e2e4","mine":true,"comments":["We open with the king's pawn: it takes a central square and opens two lines at once, one for the queen and one for the f1 bishop toward c4 and f7. Every line in this course starts here, and our idea never changes: develop fast and give Black concrete problems to solve early."],"shapes":[],"ply":1},{"san":"e5","uci":"e7e5","fenBefore":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1","fenAfter":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","key":"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -|e7e5","mine":false,"comments":["Black answers in kind and claims the centre with a pawn of their own. It is the most common reply, and it leaves f7 defended only by the king, which is the starting point of this chapter."],"shapes":[],"ply":2},{"san":"Nf3","uci":"g1f3","fenBefore":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2","fenAfter":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 2","key":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -|g1f3","mine":true,"comments":["Our knight develops with a threat: e5 is attacked and nothing defends it yet. From f3 it also covers d4 and can jump to g5, the square we will use to hit f7. Black usually spends the next move protecting e5."],"shapes":[],"ply":3},{"san":"Nc6","uci":"b8c6","fenBefore":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 2","fenAfter":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3","key":"rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq -|b8c6","mine":false,"comments":["The natural answer: the knight guards e5 and adds control of d4, the square our d-pawn would like to use. Now we bring the bishop out with a clear target in mind."],"shapes":[],"ply":4},{"san":"Bc4","uci":"f1c4","fenBefore":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3","fenAfter":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3","key":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq -|f1c4","mine":true,"comments":["We put the bishop on the a2-g8 diagonal, where it presses on d5 and aims straight at f7. That pawn has only the king to protect it, so every extra attacker creates tactics. Black's two main replies are 3...Nf6, this chapter, and 3...Bc5, the next one."],"shapes":[],"ply":5},{"san":"Nf6","uci":"g8f6","fenBefore":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 4 4","key":"r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -|g8f6","mine":false,"comments":["The Two Knights Defence. Black develops quickly and hits e4, ready for a fight. The cost is concrete: the knight on f6 cuts the d8 queen off from g5, so our knight can jump there and double the attack on f7."],"shapes":[],"ply":6},{"san":"Ng5","uci":"f3g5","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 4 4","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 5 4","key":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -|f3g5","mine":true,"comments":["We jump to g5, and now knight and bishop both hit f7, which only the king defends. Our e4 pawn is left hanging, but that does not matter: the threat is Nxf7, forking the queen on d8 and the rook on h8. The central defence is ...d5, blocking the bishop and attacking it."],"shapes":[],"ply":7},{"san":"d5","uci":"d7d5","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 5 4","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 5","key":"r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -|d7d5","mine":false,"comments":["The main defence: the pawn cuts the c4 bishop off from f7 and attacks it. Slower moves lose material; after 4...h6, for example, 5.Nxf7 forks the queen and the h8 rook. We take on d5, and the question about f7 comes straight back."],"shapes":[],"ply":8},{"san":"exd5","uci":"e4d5","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 5","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3Pp1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -|e4d5","mine":true,"comments":["We take the central pawn. Black now chooses how to win it back: the natural 5...Nxd5 puts a knight on d5, right in front of our bishop and easy to gang up on, while 5...Na5 gives the pawn up for time and is covered in the second half of the chapter."],"shapes":[],"ply":9},{"san":"Nxd5","uci":"f6d5","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3Pp1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 5","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n5/3np1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 6","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3Pp1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -|f6d5","mine":false,"comments":["The obvious recapture, and a well-known mistake. The knight on d5 blocks the bishop's path to f7, but it becomes a target itself: once the king is dragged to f7, the knight is pinned in front of it."],"shapes":[],"ply":10},{"san":"Nxf7","uci":"g5f7","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n5/3np1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 6","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2Npp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 6","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n5/3np1N1/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq -|g5f7","mine":true,"comments":["The Fried Liver sacrifice. The knight takes on f7 and forks the queen and the h8 rook, so Black can hardly avoid taking it with the king. We give a knight for a pawn to pull the king forward, where the d5 knight in front of it is pinned by our bishop."],"shapes":[],"ply":11},{"san":"Kxf7","uci":"e8f7","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2Npp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 6","fenAfter":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQ - 0 7","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp2Npp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq -|e8f7","mine":false,"comments":["Black accepts the piece. The king now stands on f7 with the d5 knight pinned to it by our c4 bishop, and that pin is what our next move exploits."],"shapes":[],"ply":12},{"san":"Qf3+","uci":"d1f3","fenBefore":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQ - 0 7","fenAfter":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 1 7","key":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQ -|d1f3","mine":true,"comments":["Check, and a second attacker on the pinned d5 knight. The king has no comfortable square: going back to e8 leaves the knight short of defenders, while stepping up to e6 keeps it guarded at the price of walking into the middle of the board. Either way we win material back or keep a lasting attack."],"shapes":[],"ply":13},{"san":"Ke8","uci":"f7e8","fenBefore":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 1 7","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 2 8","key":"r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ -|f7e8","mine":false,"comments":["The king runs home, which looks safe but gives up the defence of d5. The knight is now attacked by our bishop and queen and defended only by the d8 queen."],"shapes":[],"ply":14},{"san":"Bxd5","uci":"c4d5","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 2 8","fenAfter":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 8","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ -|c4d5","mine":true,"comments":["We take the knight, and the bishop on d5 supports a new threat: Qf7 mate. It also keeps pressure on the c6 knight, so Black has to defend f7 and keep an eye on c6 at the same time."],"shapes":[],"ply":15},{"san":"Qf6","uci":"d8f6","fenBefore":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 8","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2n2q2/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 1 9","key":"r1bqkb1r/ppp3pp/2n5/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ -|d8f6","mine":false,"comments":["The most natural defence: the queen covers f7 and offers a trade on f3, which would end our attack. We use the moment to remove the c6 knight first."],"shapes":[],"ply":16},{"san":"Bxc6+","uci":"d5c6","fenBefore":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2n2q2/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 1 9","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2B2q2/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 9","key":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2n2q2/3Bp3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ -|d5c6","mine":true,"comments":["We remove the defender with check before anything else. A trade on f3 would have spoiled our kingside pawns; this way the queens come off on c6 instead, and we keep the extra pawn from the opening."],"shapes":[],"ply":17},{"san":"Qxc6","uci":"f6c6","fenBefore":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2B2q2/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 9","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2q5/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 0 10","key":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2B2q2/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ -|f6c6","mine":false,"comments":[],"shapes":[],"ply":18},{"san":"Qxc6+","uci":"f3c6","fenBefore":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2q5/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 0 10","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2Q5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 10","key":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2q5/4p3/8/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ -|f3c6","mine":true,"comments":["We trade queens with check. The attack is over, but what remains is a simple ending we are glad to play: a pawn up, with Black's queenside pawns broken."],"shapes":[],"ply":19},{"san":"bxc6","uci":"b7c6","fenBefore":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2Q5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 10","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/p1p3pp/2p5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 0 11","key":"r1b1kb1r/ppp3pp/2Q5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ -|b7c6","mine":false,"comments":[],"shapes":[],"ply":20},{"san":"d3","uci":"d2d3","fenBefore":"r1b1kb1r/p1p3pp/2p5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ - 0 11","fenAfter":"r1b1kb1r/p1p3pp/2p5/4p3/8/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 0 11","key":"r1b1kb1r/p1p3pp/2p5/4p3/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R w KQ -|d2d3","mine":true,"comments":["Where we are: a clean pawn up, and Black's queenside pawns are split into an isolated a-pawn and doubled c-pawns, long-term targets. Black has the two bishops, which is the only real compensation. Our plan is calm development: Nc3 or Nd2, Be3, castle, then rooks to the e- and d-files and pressure on c6 and e5. We trade pieces whenever we can; each trade makes the extra pawn count more."],"shapes":[],"ply":21}]}],"learn":["0.0"],"review":[],"known":[],"next":{"lineId":"0.1","locked":true}}